scope: |
  `f.pow` in v2's Core IR kernel, so v3's `math.pow` stops being partial. The owner's decision of
  2026-08-14, taken after I brought him the boundary question rather than deciding it myself.

  I TOLD HIM "ONE LINE" AND THAT WAS WRONG — measured after, and this claim exists at the size the
  measurement showed. `f.sqrt` is implemented at SIX sites, not one: `v2/src/Runtime.scala` (the
  VM), the js, jvm, rust and swift backends, plus swift's ALLOWED-PRIM LIST, and it is named in
  `v2/specs/10-core-ir.md`. A prim added only to the VM runs on v3's bridge and dies on any other
  backend compiling the same Core IR.

  AND THE SPEC ARGUES AGAINST IT, which is why this was a question and not a task: line 338 says
  "(transcendentals such as `sin`/`cos`/`log`/`exp` live in the `Mira` prelude, not the kernel)".
  `pow` is not in that list, `sqrt` IS in the kernel and is exactly `pow(x, ½)`, and no `f.exp` /
  `f.log` / `f.pow` exists anywhere in the tree — the Mira prelude is an intention in that
  parenthetical, not code. The owner chose the kernel. The spec line gets NARROWED to say what it
  now means rather than left to contradict the code.

  WHY NO v3-ONLY FIX WORKS, measured before asking: the reference lane is libm-exact
  (`pow(2.0, 0.1)` = 1.0717734625362931, bit for bit JVM's `Math.pow`), so a ScalaScript series or
  the repeated-sqrt expansion — tempting since both lanes have `f.sqrt` — differs in the last bits.
  And v3 cannot do it on one lane: the bridge emits a prim to v2.

  PLAN. Each backend gets its own spelling of one libm call, beside its `f.sqrt`:
    Runtime.scala   FloatV(math.pow(flt(a,0), flt(a,1)))
    JvmBackend      math.pow(_asDouble(a0), _asDouble(a1))
    JsBackend       Math.pow(a0, a1)
    RustBackend     V::Float(as_float(a0).powf(as_float(a1)))
    SwiftRuntime    .float(pow(float(args,0), float(args,1)))
    SwiftBackend    add "f.pow" to the allowed list — a list is exactly what gets forgotten
    10-core-ir.md   list it, and narrow the transcendentals parenthetical
  Then v3: `hostPrims` entry, an `Exec` case, and the prelude's `pow` delegating with `.toDouble`
  on BOTH arguments — v2's `flt` refuses an Int, measured this afternoon.

  MEASUREMENT: v3's two lanes must agree on fractional, integer, negative and zero exponents, and
  must match the REFERENCE lane bit for bit — `pow(2.0, 0.1)` is the row that fails if anyone ever
  swaps in an approximation. N with floors DIFF 3 / CRASH 9; no corpus case uses a fractional
  exponent, so N is expected to hold, not rise.
next: v2 kernel + five backends + spec, then the v3 side, then parity probes
